\documentclass{article}
\title{Leader Election Stability in Raft Consensus}
\author{M. Okonkwo and L. Ferreira}
\date{March 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Replicated state machines keep a service available even when individual servers crash.
The Raft consensus algorithm organises replication around a single elected leader,
and the stability of that leader largely determines the throughput of the cluster.

\section{Election Timing}
A leader survives only while it can exchange heartbeat messages with a majority of peers.
When network partitions are frequent, clusters spend a large fraction of their time
electing new leaders instead of serving client requests.

\section{Outlook}
We plan to evaluate randomised election timeouts under realistic wide-area latency
distributions, with particular attention to split votes during partial partitions.

